# Pattern Classification

To understand how LLMs generate ideas, outputs were broken down into core conceptual structures.

Each idea was reduced to its underlying function rather than surface wording.

Example

"AI-powered personalized learning system"  
Core: Personalization + System

"Mobility buddy program"  
Core: Social coordination

"Mycelium-inspired learning networks"  
 Core: Network system

Final Pattern Categories

The following patterns were identified across all models:

1. Optimization  
→ Improving existing systems  
(e.g. smart crosswalks, adaptive learning)

2. Platform / System  
→ Creating structured systems or networks  
(e.g. learning hubs, mobility systems)

3. Social Systems  
→ Human coordination or interaction  
(e.g. buddy systems, guilds)

4. Network / Flow  
→ Distributed or flow-based systems  
(e.g. mycelium networks, swarm systems)

5. Environmental Design  
→ Physical or spatial changes  
(e.g. sidewalks, corridors, benches)

6. Tech Integration  
→ Use of AI, sensors, digital tools  
(e.g. AI tutors, smart infrastructure)

Observation

Different ideas often mapped to the same pattern despite different wording.

This suggests repetition at the structural level, not just at the surface level.
